**Pattern:** Offline sweep + min-heap

**Key insight:** Sort intervals and queries; push intervals that started, pop those that ended, heap top is the answer.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] minInterval(int[][] intervals, int[] queries) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        Integer[] idx = new Integer[queries.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < idx.length; i++) idx[i] = i;
        Arrays.sort(idx, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(queries[a], queries[b]));
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);
        int[] res = new int[queries.length];
        int i = 0;
        for (int qi : idx) {
            int q = queries[qi];
            while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= q) {
                pq.add(new int[]{intervals[i][1] - intervals[i][0] + 1, intervals[i][1]});
                i++;
            }
            while (!pq.isEmpty() && pq.peek()[1] < q) pq.poll();
            res[qi] = pq.isEmpty() ? -1 : pq.peek()[0];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

